No, it's just gaining levels that costs more, not finding better armour.
The discussion was about getting to mine town at level one, which
implies using the protectoin racket.

> If you rely on your pet to test cursedness of armor, then you don't
> even need an altar.  (Although I find that this may take too long, if
> you are playing a class that does not start with food.)

In my opinion, it doesn't take long at all. I usually wait until I have
at least six pieces of armour or so, and drop them in a room around me:

   |-------|
   |...[[..|
   |..[@[.f|
   |...[[..|
   |-------|

Now I just wait and whenever my pet steps on and off something, I pick
it up. Once it is no longer stepping on the remaining items, I pick
those up and mark them "cursed". Just to double check, I drop them in a
corridor and see if my pet follows me (it will in the end even step on
cursed objects, since it will do everything it can to follow you, so
don't rely on this to be certain).

A safer way to test this is if you have more than one known cursed
items, and one item you wish to test. Drop the items as follows:

   |-------|
   |....[..|
   |...@[.f|
   |....[..|
   |-------|

The objects above shoud be cursed but for one object you're testing for
cursedness. If there is a non-cursed object, your pet will easily reach
you. If all three objects are cursed, it will stay on the other side of
the [-line.

> I am usually too impatient to run the protection racket.

It seems you're to impatient for lots of things...  :-)

Really, I believe the *key* to winning NetHack is patience. If you don't
take the time to play carefully, I think you'll never win. Only after
your first ten ascensions or so you might become less careful, and rely
more on experience, which can make up for small mistakes.

